              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_INSERT
            }
            lines {
              text: "    originalPassage: `The supremacy of civilian authority over the military establishment is one of the foundational hallmarks of a constitutional democracy. In democratic governance, the primary role of the armed forces and security apparatus is to safeguard territorial sovereignty and preserve domestic peace against existential threats, strictly within the constitutional perimeter. However, this civilian oversight must not degenerate into bureaucratic micromanagement or the politicization of operational doctrines. When security establishments are insulated from transient electoral pressures while remaining strictly accountable to the elected legislature, national security policy achieves both strategic continuity and moral legitimacy. Historical experience across post-colonial republics demonstrates that democracies falter when the boundary between political leadership and uniformed commands becomes porous. Therefore, a modern democracy requires institutionalized advisory channels, professionalized defence councils, and rigorous legislative oversight that respect operational military expertise without ceding the ultimate decision-making power of the sovereign people. True military effectiveness in the 21st century is inseparable from the democratic values that the armed forces are sworn to protect.`,"
