    session_summary: "<summary>\n1. **Task Overview**\n   - **User Request**: Add a configuration UI in the AI Tutor component (`src/components/AiTopicBreakdown.tsx`) that allows users to manually toggle the \"Statement Depth\" for question generation (ranging from simple single-statement factual recall [depth 1] to 4-statement UPSC-style complex analysis [depth 4]), and update the backend AI generation logic in `server.ts` to respect this setting.\n   - **Target Application**: \"DutyDeck\" - UPSC CAPF AC & SSC CPO Dual Prep web application running on `http://localhost:3000`.\n   - **Target Statement Depths**:\n     - Depth 1: Single-statement factual recall (Direct CBT objective format, no numbered statement list, type: `SINGLE_CHOICE`).\n     - Depth 2: Binary contrast / foundational verification (`1 only`, `2 only`, `Both 1 and 2`, `Neither 1 nor 2`).\n     - Depth 3: Canonical 3-statement UPSC CAPF AC elimination (`1 and 2 only`, `2 and 3 only`, etc.).\n     - Depth 4: Complex 4-statement multi-layered UPSC elimination (`1, 2 and 4 only`, etc.).\n\n2. **Progress**\n   - **Completed in this turn**:\n     - `src/types/index.ts`: Updated `statementDepth` from `2 | 3 | 4` to `1 | 2 | 3 | 4` in `TopicProficiency` and `AdaptiveAssessment`. Added `\'Factual Recall\'` to `AdaptiveComplexityLevel`.\n     - `src/utils/adaptiveEngine.ts`: Added assessment logic for `statementDepth === 1` / `\'Factual Recall\'` in `determineAdaptiveAssessment` and added prompt directives for single-statement factual recall in `buildAdaptivePromptInstruction`.\n   - **Previously completed and verified**:\n     - Initial DutyDeck application with Nordic Botanical theme (`#F7F8F5`, `#1E252B`, `#36624B`, `#E3EFE8`, `#2F7C7E`, `#D97746`).\n     - CBT Speed Mode with 15-minute sectional lock and >45s speed alerts.\n     - CAPF Multi-statement Elimination Engine with interactive scratchpad and auto-deduction.\n     - Paper 2 Argument & Structure Lab with 20-minute timed markdown editor and Gemini rubric evaluation.\n     - AI Syllabus Tutor with ELI5, UPSC depth, CPO shortcuts, and adaptive question generation.\n     - Mistake Vault with SRS review scheduling (1, 3, 7 days) and error taxonomy.\n     - 10 passing unit tests in `src/utils/rules.test.ts` and `src/utils/adaptiveEngine.test.ts`.\n\n3. **Key Findings**\n   - When `statementDepth === 1`:\n     - Questions must NOT render numbered statement lists; instead, `q.questionText` is the direct question and `statements` is empty or undefined. Options are 4 direct answers A, B, C, D.\n     - The AI prompt directive must specify `type: SINGLE_CHOICE` and no numbered statements array.\n     - Fallback questions for depth 1 should provide direct factual recall questions (e.g., Article numbers, specific constitutional amendments, foundational facts).\n   - In `AiTopicBreakdown.tsx`, the depth selector previously showed `[2, 3, 4]`. It must be updated to `[1, 2, 3, 4]` with dedicated labels:\n     - `1: \"1 Stmt (Factual Recall)\"`\n     - `2: \"2 Stmts (Foundational)\"`\n     - `3: \"3 Stmts (Standard UPSC)\"`\n     - `4: \"4 Stmts (Complex Analysis)\"`\n\n4. **Errors & Fixes**\n   - **Error 1**: `PERMISSION_DENIED` (403) from Gemini API due to leaked test API key in earlier turn.\n     - *Fix*: Created a robust curriculum knowledge engine fallback in `server.ts` so all endpoints (`/api/ai/breakdown`, `/api/ai/generate-questions`, `/api/ai/evaluate-paper2`, `/api/ai/diagnose-mistake`) return structured curriculum content and never fail or 500 when external API keys are unavailable.\n   - **Error 2**: `server.ts(463,3): error TS1128: Declaration or statement expected` caused by a duplicate catch block during a code replace.\n     - *Fix*: Cleanly removed lines 463-553 in `server.ts`. Compile and lint passed with 0 errors.\n   - **Error 3**: Playwright strict mode violation: `locator(\'button:has-text(\"Mistake Vault\")\')` matched 2 elements.\n     - *Fix*: Disambiguated locator to `button:has-text(\"Open Mistake Vault\")`.\n\n5. **Active Context**\n   - Dev server runs on port 3000 via `server.ts` using Express + Vite middleware.\n   - `src/types/index.ts` and `src/utils/adaptiveEngine.ts` already support depth 1.\n   - Next files needing immediate updates: `server.ts` (fallback for depth 1 questions), `src/components/AiTopicBreakdown.tsx` (the UI toggle and state wiring for depths 1-4), and `src/utils/adaptiveEngine.test.ts` (unit tests for depth 1).\n\n6. **Next Steps**\n   1. **Update `server.ts`**:\n      - In `/api/ai/generate-questions`, add the `statementDepth === 1` fallback block generating high-yield 1-statement factual recall questions (e.g. direct CBT questions with 4 options and no statement list).\n      - Ensure `statementDepth === 1` rules in system instruction specify direct factual question format.\n   2. **Update `src/components/AiTopicBreakdown.tsx`**:\n      - Update `manualDepth` state to type `1 | 2 | 3 | 4` (default 3 or matching auto).\n      - In the statement depth configuration section, render 4 buttons: `1 Stmt (Factual)`, `2 Stmts (Foundational)`, `3 Stmts (Standard UPSC)`, `4 Stmts (Complex Analysis)`.\n      - Allow the user to click any of the 4 depths directly (either in manual mode or clicking immediately switches to manual depth).\n      - Update the description helper:\n        - 1: \"Direct CBT factual recall (single-statement, direct options A-D)\"\n        - 2: \"Remedial / Binary contrast (Statements 1 & 2)\"\n        - 3: \"Canonical UPSC CAPF elimination (Statements 1, 2, 3)\"\n        - 4: \"Advanced multi-combinatorial analysis (Statements 1, 2, 3, 4)\"\n      - Ensure the \"Generate Drill\" button and payload pass the selected depth (1, 2, 3, or 4) to `/api/ai/generate-questions`.\n      - Ensure rendering of generated drill handles 1-statement questions cleanly (only render statement list if `statements && statements.length > 0`).\n   3. **Update unit tests (`src/utils/adaptiveEngine.test.ts`)**:\n      - Add test for `statementDepth === 1` (\"Factual Recall\") assessment and prompt instruction.\n      - Run `npx --yes tsx --test src/utils/rules.test.ts src/utils/adaptiveEngine.test.ts`.\n   4. **Compile & Lint Verification**:\n      - Run `compile_applet` and `lint_applet`.\n      - Run `restart_dev_server`.\n   5. **Browser Verification (`playwright-cli`)**:\n      - Run 1 targeted verification round via `playwright-cli`:\n        - Open `http://localhost:3000`, go to \"AI Syllabus Tutor\".\n        - Click the \"1 Stmt\" toggle. Verify button reflects depth 1.\n        - Click \"Generate 1-Statement Drill\".\n        - Verify rendered question has 0 bullet statements and is a direct single-statement question with 4 options.\n        - Click the \"4 Stmts\" toggle and generate a 4-statement drill to verify complex depth.\n        - Capture screenshot to `.playwright-cli/r14-depth-toggle.png`.\n\n7. **Commitments & Constraints**\n   - Nordic Botanical theme tokens must be preserved.\n   - All pure logic changes must be covered by `node:test` unit tests.\n   - Dev server must run on port 3000 via `server.ts`.\n   - Never expose API keys or create UI for API keys.\n</summary>"
    included_step_index_end: 167
    render_latest_checkpoint: true
    total_subagent_count: 0
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "Here is the full initial file tree for the application:\n/.env.example\n/.gitignore\n/index.html\n/metadata.json\n/package.json\n/src/App.tsx\n/src/index.css\n/src/main.tsx\n/tsconfig.json\n/vite.config.ts\n\nThe following dependencies are installed:\n@google/genai: ^2.4.0\n@tailwindcss/vite: ^4.3.3\n@vitejs/plugin-react: ^6.1.1\ndotenv: ^17.2.3\nexpress: ^4.21.2\nlucide-react: ^0.546.0\nmotion: ^12.23.24\nreact: ^19.0.1\nreact-dom: ^19.0.1\nvite: ^8.3.0\n\nThe following devDependencies are installed:\n@types/express: ^4.17.21\n@types/node: ^22.14.0\n@types/react: ^19.3.0\n@types/react-dom: ^19.3.0\nautoprefixer: ^10.4.21\nesbuild: ^0.25.0\ntailwindcss: ^4.3.3\ntsx: ^4.21.0\ntypescript: ^7.0.2\n\nKey file contents:\n\n--- /.env.example ---\n```\n# GEMINI_API_KEY: Required for Gemini AI API calls.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime from user secrets.\n# Users configure this via the Secrets panel in the AI Studio UI.\nGEMINI_API_KEY=\"MY_GEMINI_API_KEY\"\n\n# APP_URL: The URL where this applet is hosted.\n# AI Studio automatically injects this at runtime with the Cloud Run service URL.\n# Used for self-referential links, OAuth callbacks, and API endpoints.\nAPP_URL=\"MY_APP_URL\"\n\n```\n\n--- /index.html ---\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n    <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n    <title>My Google AI Studio App</title>\n    <meta name=\"description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"My Google AI Studio App\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:description\" content=\"An application built with Google AI Studio.\" />\n    <meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"website\" />\n    <meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\" />\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <div id=\"root\"></div>\n    <script type=\"module\" src=\"/src/main.tsx\"></script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n\n\n```\n\n--- /metadata.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"\",\n  \"description\": \"\",\n  \"requestFramePermissions\": [],\n  \"majorCapabilities\": [\"MAJOR_CAPABILITY_SERVER_SIDE_GEMINI_API\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/App.tsx ---\n```typescript\n/**\n * @license\n * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0\n */\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return <div></div>;\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /src/index.css ---\n```css\n@import \"tailwindcss\";\n\n```\n\n--- /src/main.tsx ---\n```typescript\nimport {createRoot} from \'react-dom/client\';\nimport App from \'./App.tsx\';\nimport \'./index.css\';\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(\'root\')!).render(<App />);\n\n```\n\n--- /tsconfig.json ---\n```json\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n    \"experimentalDecorators\": true,\n    \"useDefineForClassFields\": false,\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"types\": [\"vite/client\"],\n    \"lib\": [\n      \"ES2022\",\n      \"DOM\",\n      \"DOM.Iterable\"\n    ],\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\",\n    \"isolatedModules\": true,\n    \"moduleDetection\": \"force\",\n    \"allowJs\": true,\n    \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\",\n    \"paths\": {\n      \"@/*\": [\n        \"./*\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"allowImportingTsExtensions\": true,\n    \"noEmit\": true\n  },\n  \"exclude\": [\"dist\"]\n}\n\n```\n\n--- /vite.config.ts ---\n```typescript\nimport tailwindcss from \'@tailwindcss/vite\';\nimport react from \'@vitejs/plugin-react\';\nimport path from \'path\';\nimport {defineConfig} from \'vite\';\n\nexport default defineConfig(() => {\n  return {\n    plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()],\n    resolve: {\n      alias: {\n        \'@\': path.resolve(__dirname, \'.\'),\n      },\n    },\n    server: {\n      // HMR is disabled in AI Studio via DISABLE_HMR env var.\n      // Do not modify\342\200\224file watching is disabled to prevent flickering during agent edits.\n      hmr: process.env.DISABLE_HMR !== \'true\',\n      // Disable file watching when DISABLE_HMR is true to save CPU during agent edits.\n      watch: process.env.DISABLE_HMR === \'true\' ? null : {},\n    },\n  };\n});\n\n```"
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "A dedicated prep web app for dual UPSC CAPF AC and SSC CPO aspirants must bridge two distinct exam demands: the high-speed objective arithmetic/reasoning accuracy required by SSC CPO (100 marks / 60 minutes with 15-minute sectional gates), and the multi-statement conceptual depth plus subjective argument writing required by UPSC CAPF Paper 1 and Paper 2.\n\nBelow is an end-to-end product breakdown, core architecture, and feature roadmap designed specifically for this dual-target strategy.\n\n\n1. Core Product Architecture & Concept: \"DutyDeck\"\nInstead of building a generic quiz platform, structure the web app around the overlap and divergence of the two exams:\n\n\n- The Shared Core: Polity, Modern History, Geography, General Science, and English Comprehension.\n- \n- The CPO Track: High-speed Quant, fast Logical Reasoning, and rapid English vocabulary drills under strict time pressure.\n- \n- The CAPF Track: Multi-statement elimination MCQs (UPSC standard) and Paper 2 subjective writing (Essay, Counter-Argument, Report, Pr\303\251cis).\n- \n2. High-Impact Feature Breakdown\nA. Dual-Mode Question Engine\n- CPO Speed Mode (Sprint):\n- \n  - Strict 15-minute auto-locking sectional timer replicating the Paper 1 CBT pattern.\n  - \n  - Speed tracking per question (e.g., flag questions taking $>45$ seconds).\n  - \n  - Direct formula and shortcut breakdown on the answer screen.\n  - \n- CAPF Deep-Dive Mode (Analytical):\n- \n  - Support for multi-statement objective questions (\"Which of the statements given above is/are correct? 1 and 2 only, 2 and 3...\").\n  - \n  - Option elimination scratchpad (lets you cross out individual statements to practice elimination techniques).\n  - \n  - Citation tags linking directly to standard sources (e.g., M. Laxmikanth Ch. 12, Spectrum Ch. 8, NCERT Class 11 Geo).\n  - \nB. CAPF Paper 2 \"Argument & Structure Lab\"\n- Timed Markdown Editor: Fixed 15\342\200\22320 minute timer simulating exam conditions for Essays (approx. 300 words), Counter-Arguments (For/Against), and Reports.\n- \n- Structure Scaffolding Checkers:\n- \n  - Argument Splitter: Enforces a clean 3-part split: Intro/Context -> 3 Points \"For\" -> 3 Points \"Against\" -> Balanced Conclusion.\n  - \n  - Report Formatter: Pre-formats the standard header (Date, Place, Special Correspondent) to build muscle memory.\n  - \n- LLM Evaluation API: Run submitted text against a strict evaluation prompt checking for:\n- \n  - Argument depth and constitutional/factual backing.\n  - \n  - Objective tone (flagging overly emotional or partisan language).\n  - \n  - Pr\303\251cis word count compliance (strict one-third limit).\n  - \nC. Micro-Categorized PYQ Bank & Error Log\n- Filter past questions not just by subject, but by specific micro-topics (e.g., Polity -> Emergency Provisions, Quant -> Speed, Time & Distance, Modern History -> 1919\342\200\2231935 Reforms).\n- \n- \"Mistake Vault\": Every incorrect or skipped answer is automatically saved with a prompt: \"Why did you miss this? (A: Conceptual Gap, B: Misread Question, C: Calculation/Time Panic)\".\n- \n- Revision filter that only serves questions you missed twice or tagged under Conceptual Gap.\n- \nD. Unified Syllabus & Daily Routine Tracker\n- Visual Bento-style dashboard showing percentage completion of overlapping subjects versus exam-specific modules.\n- \n- Spaced Repetition (SRS) reminders: If you study Fundamental Rights today, the app automatically serves 5 high-yield MCQs 3 days later, then 7 days later.\n- \n3. Recommended Tech Stack\nLayerRecommended ChoiceJustificationFrameworkNext.js (App Router, TypeScript)Fast page loads, server-side rendering for PYQ indexing, clean component structuring.StylingTailwind CSS + shadcn/uiClean, distraction-free aesthetic with accessible keyboard navigation (essential for speed drilling).DatabaseMongoDB / PostgreSQL (via Prisma)Store structured question schemas, JSON-based Paper 2 prompts, and user drill sessions.State / OfflineZustand + IndexedDB / LocalStorageEnables offline drill access so test-taking is instant without network lag.AI EvaluationGemini API (via Next.js API Routes)Low latency evaluation for Paper 2 essays and argument structure analysis.\n4. Database Schema Blueprint (Questions & Drills)\nTypeScript\n\n// Question Schemainterface Question {\n  id: string;\n  targetExam: (\'SSC_CPO\' | \'UPSC_CAPF\')[];\n  subject: string; // \'Polity\' | \'Quantitative Aptitude\' | \'Reasoning\' | etc.\n  microTopic: string; // \'Preamble\', \'Compound Interest\', etc.\n  difficulty: \'Speed\' | \'Analytical\';\n  type: \'SINGLE_CHOICE\' | \'STATEMENT_BASED\';\n  questionText: string;\n  statements?: string[]; // Used for UPSC 1, 2, 3 format\n  options: { key: \'A\' | \'B\' | \'C\' | \'D\'; text: string }[];\n  correctAnswer: \'A\' | \'B\' | \'C\' | \'D\';\n  explanation: {\n    coreConcept: string;\n    shortcutOrTrick?: string;\n    standardBookRef?: string;\n  };\n}// User Paper 2 Submission Schemainterface Paper2Submission {\n  userId: string;\n  topicType: \'ESSAY\' | \'ARGUMENT\' | \'REPORT\' | \'PRECIS\';\n  prompt: string;\n  content: string;\n  wordCount: number;\n  timeSpentSeconds: number;\n  aiFeedback?: {\n    structuralScore: number;\n    grammarScore: number;\n    pointsForFeedback: string[];\n    pointsAgainstFeedback: string[];\n    suggestedImprovements: string[];\n  };\n}\n5. Implementation Roadmap (Phased Build)\n1. Phase 1 (The Speed Core): Build the Next.js shell with shadcn/ui, implement the 100-question CBT timer with strict 15-minute section locking, and populate with 300\342\200\223400 CPO Quant/Reasoning/English PYQs.\n2. \n3. Phase 2 (CAPF Elimination Engine): Build the multi-statement MCQ viewer with elimination strikethroughs and source tagging (Polity/Spectrum chapters).\n4. \n5. Phase 3 (Paper 2 Desk): Introduce the split-screen markdown editor with an automated Gemini endpoint to score structure, tone, and arguments against standard UPSC rubrics.\n6. \n7. Phase 4 (Analytics & Vault): Wire up the automated mistake classification dashboard and spaced-repetition revision prompts.\n8. \nWould you like to start by generating the complete Next.js CBT test-taking component with the sectional timer, or draft the Paper 2 evaluation prompt for the backend?\n\nThe ideal visual direction for a high-focus exam app is a \"Nordic Botanical\" (or Sage & Warm Slate) theme. It replaces high-stress \"exam alert reds\" and sterile greys with muted, organic tones that keep screen fatigue low during 2-to-3-hour study blocks while retaining distinct color identities for your subjects and metrics.\n\n\n1. Palette Specifications\nRoleTone / NameHex CodeUI ApplicationCanvas (Background)Cloud Bone#F7F8F5Main viewport background; warmer and softer on the eyes than pure #FFFFFF.Surface (Cards/Panels)Soft Pure#FFFFFFQuiz cards, sidebar, writing editor; elevation via subtle borders instead of heavy drop shadows.Primary TextDeep Slate#1E252BHeadings, reading passages, Paper 2 prompts; high contrast without harsh jet black.Secondary TextMuted Moss Slate#5C646BMeta labels, timers, micro-topic tags, chapter citations.Primary AccentDeep Sage Green#36624BPrimary action buttons (\"Submit Section\", \"Start Drill\"), active tab states.Subtle Accent GlowMint Wash#E3EFE8Selected option background, active sidebar link pills.\n2. Functional Accent Colors (Calm & Balanced)\nAvoid high-voltage neon alerts. Use desaturated, mineral-inspired shades to color-code sections and progress:\n\n\n- Polity & Constitution: Nordic Teal (#2F7C7E) \342\200\224 Calm, authoritative, and distinct from the main sage green.\n- \n- Quant & Reasoning: Soft Apricot / Clay (#D97746) \342\200\224 Energizing and easy to spot without looking like an error state.\n- \n- Modern History & Geo: Olive Mustard (#8C7837) \342\200\224 Earthy and grounded for long-reading passages.\n- \n- Paper 2 Analytical / Essay: Muted Indigo (#4B5A8E) \342\200\224 Scholarly, quiet tone for essay writing and argument mapping.\n- \n- Success / Correct: Eucalyptus (#2E7D5B) with a light wash (#E6F4EA).\n- \n- Needs Review / Re-attempt: Dusty Terracotta (#C25345) with a light wash (#FBEAE8) \342\200\224 informative rather than punitive.\n- \n3. Tailwind CSS Configuration Token Set\nDrop these directly into your Tailwind configuration to maintain strict design tokens across your components:\n\nJavaScript\n\n// tailwind.config.js / globals.css color tokenstheme: {\n  extend: {\n    colors: {\n      canvas: {\n        DEFAULT: \'#F7F8F5\',\n        subtle: \'#EEF0EA\',\n      },\n      surface: {\n        DEFAULT: \'#FFFFFF\',\n        hover: \'#F2F4F0\',\n      },\n      content: {\n        primary: \'#1E252B\',\n        secondary: \'#5C646B\',\n        muted: \'#8B949E\',\n      },\n      brand: {\n        sage: \'#36624B\',\n        mint: \'#E3EFE8\',\n      },\n      track: {\n        polity: \'#2F7C7E\',\n        quant: \'#D97746\',\n        history: \'#8C7837\',\n        paper2: \'#4B5A8E\',\n      },\n      status: {\n        correct: \'#2E7D5B\',\n        correctSurface: \'#E6F4EA\',\n        review: \'#C25345\',\n        reviewSurface: \'#FBEAE8\',\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n4. UI Polish & Aesthetic Treatment\n- Borders over Shadows: Use crisp, thin borders (border border-slate-200/80 or border-[#E2E6DD]) for cards rather than heavy box shadows. It keeps the split screens (like the Paper 2 editor next to the argument guideline) clean and paper-like.\n- \n- Typography: Combine Inter or Plus Jakarta Sans for UI controls, badges, and metrics with Newsreader or Merriweather for the reading passages and Paper 2 essay prompts to replicate standard print exam sheets.\n- \n- Dark Mode Adaptation: When switching to dark mode, shift the canvas to an inky deep pine-slate (#121816) instead of true OLED black (#000000), paired with muted sage cards (#1A2420).\n- \n\n\n\nbuild this web app\n\nit should have ai integrated to lear the topic detailed syllabus and provide personalized practice questions based on specific learning gaps.\n\nand upsc capf like question \npersonal ai to help and break topic more easy\n\nit can teach very easy an generate question on the topic"
    }
