            lines {
              text: "      \'National Emergency (Art 352) transforms India from a federal system into a unitary system without any formal constitutional amendment.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Can only be proclaimed on 3 grounds: War, External Aggression, or Armed Rebellion (44th Amendment Act 1978 deleted \"Internal Disturbance\").\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Requires approval by both Houses within 1 month (Art 352) by special majority, whereas State Emergency (Art 356) and Financial Emergency (Art 360) give 2 months by simple majority.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    ],"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    upscAnalyticalDepth:"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Article 352 requires written recommendation of the Union Cabinet (ministers of cabinet rank), not merely the Prime Minister alone. Under Article 358, fundamental rights under Article 19 are automatically suspended only when emergency is declared on grounds of war/external aggression (not armed rebellion). Under Article 359, the President may suspend the right to enforce fundamental rights via court, BUT Articles 20 (protection against conviction) and 21 (right to life and personal liberty) can NEVER be suspended (44th Amendment Act 1978; Minerva Mills case 1980).\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    cpoSpeedShortcut:"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'30-Second CBT Rule: \"1-Month vs 2-Months\". Article 352 is the deadliest, so Parliament gets only 1 month to approve. Articles 356 and 360 are less urgent, so Parliament gets 2 months. Special majority is needed only for Art 352; simple majority suffices for Art 356 & 360.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    memoryHook:"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Mnemonic: \"WAR-20-21-UNTOUCHABLE\" (Grounds: War, External Aggression, Armed Rebellion; Arts 20 & 21 can never be touched even during emergency).\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    examinerTrap:"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Examiners frequently swap \"written advice of Prime Minister\" for \"written advice of Cabinet\" (it MUST be Cabinet) and claim Art 19 is suspended during Armed Rebellion (it is NOT suspended during armed rebellion).\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    recommendedChapters: \'M. Laxmikanth (7th Ed.) \342\200\224 Chapter 16: Emergency Provisions\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "  },"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "  armed_forces: {"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    topic: \'Armed Forces & Fundamental Rights (Art 33, 34, 35)\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    eli5Summary: ["
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Article 33 allows the restriction of Fundamental Rights for members of Armed Forces, CAPFs (BSF, CRPF, ITBP, CISF, SSB), and Intelligence agencies to ensure discipline and proper discharge of duties.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Only Parliament has the power to make laws under Article 33; State Legislatures have zero jurisdiction.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Martial Law (Article 34) is borrowed from the English common law and has no specific definition in the Constitution.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    ],"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    upscAnalyticalDepth:"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Under Article 33, parliamentary laws can restrict rights including freedom of speech, right to form associations/unions, and access to the press. Such laws cannot be challenged in any court for contravening Fundamental Rights. Under Article 34, Parliament can indemnify any government servant for acts done during Martial Law. Note that National Emergency affects Centre-State relations, whereas Martial Law primarily affects Fundamental Rights and ordinary civil courts.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    cpoSpeedShortcut:"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Parliament-Only Filter: Whenever a question on Art 33 or 35 mentions \"State Legislature\", cross it out immediately. It is 100% Parliament monopoly.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    memoryHook: \'Mnemonic: \"33 = Soliders Silent\" (Parliament limits soldier rights for strict operational command).\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    examinerTrap: \'Questions frequently state \"State Legislatures may also restrict rights of police forces\" \342\200\224 this is strictly false under Art 33.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    recommendedChapters: \'M. Laxmikanth \342\200\224 Chapter 7: Fundamental Rights (Articles 33 & 34)\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "  },"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "  compound_interest: {"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    topic: \'Compound vs Simple Interest (2-Yr Difference)\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    eli5Summary: ["
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Simple Interest is calculated only on the original principal every year.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Compound Interest calculates interest on the accumulated interest from preceding periods.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'For the 1st year, CI equals SI (when compounded annually). The difference begins precisely in Year 2 due to interest on Year 1 interest.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    ],"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    upscAnalyticalDepth:"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Useful for CAPF Paper 1 General Mental Ability arithmetic questions requiring rapid evaluation of compound financial instruments and depreciation schedules.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    cpoSpeedShortcut:"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Universal Formula: 2-Year Difference D = P \303\227 (R / 100)\302\262. If D = 96 and P = 15000: (R/100)\302\262 = 96/15000 = 64/10000 -> R/100 = 8/100 -> R = 8%. Solved in 15 seconds without finding individual CI/SI amounts.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    memoryHook: \'Formula: \"D = P \303\227 (R/100)\302\262\" for 2 years. For 3 years: \"D = P \303\227 (R/100)\302\262 \303\227 (3 + R/100)\".\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    examinerTrap: \'Never compute CI using P(1+R/100)^n - P and then subtract SI in CBT mode; it burns 90 seconds and leads to calculation panic.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    recommendedChapters: \'R.S. Aggarwal / SSC CPO Maths Chapterwise\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "  },"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "  goi_acts: {"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    topic: \'1909, 1919 & 1935 Government of India Acts\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    eli5Summary: ["
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'1909 Act (Morley-Minto): Introduced separate electorates for Muslims.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'1919 Act (Montagu-Chelmsford): Introduced Dyarchy in Provinces and Bicameralism at the Centre.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'1935 Act: Abolished Provincial Dyarchy, introduced Provincial Autonomy, and proposed Central Dyarchy.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    ],"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    upscAnalyticalDepth:"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'The 1919 Act divided provincial subjects into \"Transferred\" (administered by Governor with ministers responsible to legislative council) and \"Reserved\" (administered by Governor and executive council without legislative responsibility). The 1935 Act provided for an All-India Federation (which never materialized), established the Federal Court (1937), Reserve Bank of India (1935), and Federal Public Service Commission.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    cpoSpeedShortcut:"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "      \'Chronological Rule: Dyarchy was PROVINCIAL in 1919, then moved to CENTRAL in 1935 (Provincial Autonomy took its place).\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    memoryHook: \'Mnemonic: \"1919 = Provincial Split (Dyarchy); 1935 = Provincial Freedom (Autonomy)\".\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    examinerTrap: \'Confusing communal electorate expansions: 1909 was Muslims only. 1919 added Sikhs, Indian Christians, Anglo-Indians, Europeans. 1935 added Depressed Classes, Women, and Labour.\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "    recommendedChapters: \'Spectrum Modern India \342\200\224 Chapters 16 & 23\',"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "  },"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "};"
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "// 1. AI Syllabus Tutor: \"Break Topic Down Easy\""
              type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
            }
            lines {
              text: "app.post(\'/api/ai/breakdown\', async (req, res) => {"
